  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B002",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B021",
    "B015"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I change my preferred configuration to B002. Optical lattice clocks embody a clean physical mechanism: magic-wavelength confinement permits narrow atomic transitions to be used while controlling the otherwise decisive lattice-induced shift. Katori's conceptual step and Ye's defining experimental development form an unusually focused allocation of credit. The result has been independently benchmarked over many years and has become a mature instrument for precision measurement, relativistic geodesy, and fundamental tests. Compared with DMFT, this configuration more readily identifies an essential mechanism, a bounded achievement, and exact individual responsibility without diminishing its broad scientific reach.",
    "case_against": "The citation must not absorb the wider history of optical-frequency metrology, later clock-performance improvements, or a future collective redefinition of the second. A historical review should still test whether the proposed pairing fully captures the indispensable conceptual and experimental contributions. My change does not reflect support counts: DMFT remains an outstanding theoretical achievement, but its evolving framework and broad development community make a limited Nobel attribution less secure.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that Katori's magic-wavelength principle, Ye's decisive realization, and independently reproduced clock performance make B002 a mature and sharply attributable physical advance.",
        "response": "I find this comparison decisive on reassessment. The connection between the controlling principle, the experimental architecture, and quantitative validation is unusually direct. It meets the standard of an understood and reproducible mechanism, while Kroll's warning to separate it from the broader metrology enterprise should constrain the citation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson holds that lattice clocks already merit recognition without relying on a future redefinition of the second, because they are scientifically productive precision instruments with a clean two-person attribution.",
        "response": "I agree. The prize case rests on the physical invention and its demonstrated capabilities, not on a standards-policy endpoint. This also addresses my earlier preference for DMFT: a theoretical framework can be deeply transformative, but B002 has a more bounded core whose consequences have been independently established."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson values DMFT's bridge from microscopic models to measured correlated-material properties but regards its broad developer community as an attribution difficulty.",
        "response": "That distinction has led me to revise my position. DMFT isolates a powerful general treatment of local correlations and remains my leading theoretical alternative, yet its later extensions and implementations are integral to its influence. For B002, the principal concept and defining realization can be recognized more narrowly without claiming the work of the entire subsequent community."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival evidence confirms that Katori and Ye alone delimit the conceptual and experimental core of the optical-lattice-clock breakthrough.",
      "How to formulate a citation that recognizes optical lattice clocks while excluding the wider optical-clock, frequency-metrology, and standards enterprise.",
      "For B021, whether the founding DMFT construction can ultimately be separated sufficiently clearly from its later extensions for a future limited award."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No candidate-specific conflict is apparent from the supplied profile. I share a Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but no specific collaboration or connection to the proposed laureates is indicated."
  }
}
